Source: https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/rsync
Commit: 13eacd7e9a204b236310dab23682f6354ecc69bd (rsync-184)
Directory: openrsync/
License: ISC and the other notices retained in the source and LICENSE.md.

The original program is built as a static library in bx. The applet entry
point is src/applets/network/rsync/rsync.c. The Meson build runs Apple's
compile/link-only configure probes out of tree; no generated header is kept
in the repository. Upstream's Makefile also remains usable independently.

Local changes to Apple's files:
- Portable sbuf and human-readable formatting outside FreeBSD.
- Portable getopt reset, socket lengths, FTS and queue handling.
- Confined opens: Linux openat2, FreeBSD O_RESOLVE_BENEATH, otherwise a
  symlink-rejecting openat walk. The portable walk does not offer the native
  syscall's atomic confinement guarantee against concurrent directory renames.
- The bx applet re-executes its own verified executable descriptor for
  local transfers. Remote transfers still default to the remote rsync.
- All nonlocal symbols in this pinned source are namespaced in bx to avoid
  clashes with other imported libraries. The bundled rsync-modified zlib is
  also namespaced and linked for protocol-compatible compressed deltas.
- The generated FTS fallback declarations are inside the config header guard.
- The metadata permission mask uses the standard mode bits on systems without
  ALLPERMS.
- Apple-private tracing is optional when its SDK header is unavailable.

This is openrsync (protocol 29), not a GNU rsync CLI clone. In particular,
Apple's macOS metadata handling does not implement Linux ACL/xattr behavior.
The bx executable is still Linux-only due to its fetch subsystem; building
the standalone openrsync on a Unix host does not remove that restriction.
